.TH TTYSIZE 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ttysize \- print the width and height of the console
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ttysize
.RB [ w | h ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ttysize
prints the size of the console window, in character cells, on one line
on standard output.
It replaces
.B stty size
in scripts: it can print only the width, only the height, or both in any
order, and it never reports an error.
When it finds no console, it reports a width of 80 and a height of 24.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B ttysize
prints the width, a space, and the height, as in
.BR "80 24" .
Otherwise it examines each argument in turn.
An argument that begins with
.B w
prints the width, and one that begins with
.B h
prints the height.
The rest of the argument is ignored, so
.B width
means the same as
.BR w .
Any other argument prints nothing, including an empty one and one that
begins with a capital letter.
.PP
Each number is decimal.
A number printed for any argument after the first is preceded by a
space, even when the arguments before it printed nothing, so
.B ttysize x w
prints a space and the width, while
.B ttysize w x
prints the width alone.
A newline ends the output.
An argument may be repeated:
.B ttysize w w
prints the width twice.
.PP
The size comes from the console that the process is attached to, opened
as
.BR CONOUT$ ,
whatever standard input, output, and error refer to.
It is the size of the visible console window, not of the screen buffer
behind it, so scrollback is not counted.
.SH OPTIONS
.B ttysize
has no options.
It does not give
.B \-\-
special meaning, so
.B \-\-
is an argument that prints nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-help
When it is the first argument, print a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0, ignoring the other arguments.
In any other position it is an argument that prints nothing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B ttysize
reads no environment variables.
The startup code shared by all applets reads
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
and
.BR BB_UMASK ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
Only
.B BB_GLOBBING
can change what
.B ttysize
prints: unless it is
.BR 0 ,
a wildcard argument that the caller did not expand, as from
.BR cmd.exe ,
is replaced by the matching file names, and only the first letter of
each is used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Always, including when no size could be found and the default size is
printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Save the width and height in shell variables:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cols=$(ttysize w)
$ rows=$(ttysize h)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Wrap a file at spaces to fit the console:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ fold \-s \-w "$(ttysize w)" notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the height, then the width:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ttysize h w
.EE
.RE
.PP
Record the size in a file; redirection does not affect it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ttysize >size.txt 2>&1
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B stty size
prints the height first, not the width, and fails with an error when
the process has no console; see
.BR stty (1).
.PP
The usage message says the size is that of the stdin tty, but on Windows
the console is found the same way for every descriptor, so redirection
makes no difference.
Only a process with no console at all, such as one started detached
from any console, gets the default size.
The default size cannot be told from a real 80 by 24 console.
.PP
The output ends in LF, not CRLF.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR stty (1)
